<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matopos Hills</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Matopos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Matopos Hills, or Matobo Hills, are a granite landscape south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulawayo">Bulawayo</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. They were the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwari-cult">Mwari</a> religious cult, whose oracular shrines gave ritual legitimacy to the rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire</a>. The cult also served as a channel between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changamire">Changamire</a>'s court and plateau communities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matopos-hills.jpg" alt="Matopos Hills" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Macvivo at English Wikipedia (Original text: Samwise Gamgee) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunrise_Matobo_Zimbabwe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
